   "id": "bekhorot.49b.6",
   "daf": "49b",
   "sentenceId": 5,
   "he": "הוּא לִפְדּוֹת וּבְנוֹ לִפְדּוֹת — הוּא קוֹדֵם לִבְנוֹ וְכוּ׳. תָּנוּ רַבָּנַן: הוּא לִפְדּוֹת וּבְנוֹ לִפְדּוֹת — הוּא קוֹדֵם לִבְנוֹ. רַבִּי יְהוּדָה אוֹמֵר: בְּנוֹ קוֹדְמוֹ, שֶׁמִּצְוָתוֹ עַל אָבִיו, וּמִצְוַת בְּנוֹ עָלָיו.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that if one had both «himself to redeem and his son to redeem, his» own redemption «takes precedence over» that of «his son. The Sages taught» in a baraita: If one had both «himself to redeem and his son to redeem, his» own redemption «takes precedence over» that of «his son. Rabbi Yehuda says:» The redemption of «his son takes precedence, as the mitzva» to redeem the father is incumbent «upon his» own «father, and the mitzva» to redeem «his son» is incumbent «upon him.» Consequently, he should first fulfill the mitzva that is incumbent upon him by redeeming his son."
  }
 ],
